From f23ca94c8a96123ef61f54089a12719712003073 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 18:15:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 266/268] trans: rotate_left/rotate_right reduce the shift
 modulo the width; narrow ctlz counts from its own width

skim 5.7.3 `sk` at runtime (tokio 1.53.1): "thread 'tokio-rt-worker' panicked at :0:0:
assertion `left == right` failed left: 0 right: 5", Abort trap. The worker's backtrace ends
in runtime/time/wheel/level.rs Level::next_expiration, whose only equality check is
`debug_assert_eq!(self.level, NUM_LEVELS - 1)`: a level-0 slot computed a deadline in the
past. next_occupied_slot does `occupied.rotate_right(now_slot as u32)` with now_slot = the
elapsed milliseconds.

The C backend emitted a rotate as `(v >> shift) | (v << (64 - shift))` without reducing the
shift modulo the width. Rust rotates by `n % BITS`; a C shift by the width or more is
undefined. x86 masks shift counts in hardware and hid it; 32-bit PowerPC's 64-bit shift
sequences return garbage, so every timer wheel lookup after 64 ms picked a wrong slot.

Two more problems in the same emission, found by the kit:
- rotate_right's block-local temporary was called `rv`, which is also the name of the
  function's return slot. When the intrinsic's destination was the return value the result
  became `rv = rv;` and every non-inlined `uN::rotate_right` returned garbage. `-O` builds
  mostly inline it into a temporary, which is why it went unnoticed.
- `ctlz` on u8/u16 used `__builtin_clz` of the promoted 32-bit value: `1u8.leading_zeros()`
  was 31 instead of 7.

One emission now covers every width: `shift & (BITS-1)` and `(BITS - shift) & (BITS-1)`
(a zero shift no longer shifts by the width), temporaries rot_v/rot_s, the emulated u128 handles
0, 64 and more than 64 explicitly, the native u128 masks, and the narrow ctlz subtracts
`32 - BITS`.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
